WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.365
<v Examiner>Exam case 4.

2
00:00:02.365 --> 00:00:03.479
<v Examiner>Panic attacks.

3
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:06.253
<v Examiner>Manaia lives with an anxiety disorder,

4
00:00:06.253 --> 00:00:10.412
<v Examiner>which sometimes shows up as panic attacks that affect her breathing.

5
00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:15.671
<v Examiner>She describes a panic attack as a time when she breathes really, really hard and fast,

6
00:00:15.671 --> 00:00:18.056
<v Examiner>and can't control her breathing at all.

7
00:00:18.056 --> 00:00:21.541
<v Examiner>There's a link between breathing and the pH of the blood,

8
00:00:21.541 --> 00:00:25.639
<v Examiner>because the respiratory system is one of the body's buffer systems.

9
00:00:25.639 --> 00:00:30.653
<v Examiner>During a prolonged panic attack, Manaia might be at risk of respiratory alkalosis,

10
00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:32.610
<v Examiner>and you'll need to remember why.

11
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.557
Case 4 is the other big one, so it's three tracks as well.

12
00:00:36.557 --> 00:00:38.607
First, the mechanics of breathing.

13
00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:39.761
<v Examiner>Four marks.

14
00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:43.281
<v Examiner>Bullet point the steps involved in Manaia's inhalation.

15
00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:46.525
The hook: contract, bigger, lower pressure, air in.

16
00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:49.711
The diaphragm contracts, so its dome lowers.

17
00:00:49.711 --> 00:00:54.064
The external intercostal muscles contract, moving the ribs up and out.

18
00:00:54.064 --> 00:00:59.473
That enlarges the thoracic cavity, and reduces the pressure in the intrapleural cavity.

19
00:00:59.473 --> 00:01:00.655
That's Boyle's law.

20
00:01:00.655 --> 00:01:04.199
The lungs follow, so the intrapulmonary volume increases,

21
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.562
and the intrapulmonary pressure falls.

22
00:01:06.562 --> 00:01:10.293
Air flows in from the atmosphere, from high pressure to low.

23
00:01:11.743 --> 00:01:12.457
<v Examiner>Four marks.

24
00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.031
<v Examiner>Bullet point the steps involved in Manaia's exhalation.

25
00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:19.403
The hook: relax, recoil, higher pressure, air out.

26
00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:22.595
The diaphragm relaxes, so its dome rises.

27
00:01:22.595 --> 00:01:26.740
The external intercostals relax, so the ribs move down and in.

28
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.487
The elastic lungs recoil passively, reducing the intrapulmonary volume.

29
00:01:31.487 --> 00:01:34.764
Intrapulmonary pressure rises: Boyle's law again.

30
00:01:34.764 --> 00:01:36.836
And air flows out of the lungs,

31
00:01:36.836 --> 00:01:41.784
from the higher intrapulmonary pressure to the lower atmospheric pressure.

32
00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:43.884
<v Examiner>Two marks.

33
00:01:43.884 --> 00:01:46.482
<v Examiner>Manaia feels tired after a panic attack,

34
00:01:46.482 --> 00:01:49.859
<v Examiner>because forced breathing takes more muscular effort.

35
00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:52.652
<v Examiner>Which muscles are used in forced breathing?

36
00:01:52.652 --> 00:01:53.431
<v Examiner>Be specific.

37
00:01:53.881 --> 00:01:54.722
Breathing in.

38
00:01:54.722 --> 00:01:58.731
At rest, the diaphragm and the external intercostals contract.

39
00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:04.292
When it's forced, the neck muscles join in: the sternocleidomastoid, and the scalenes.

40
00:02:04.742 --> 00:02:05.651
Breathing out.

41
00:02:05.651 --> 00:02:10.388
At rest it's passive: the diaphragm and external intercostals just relax.

42
00:02:10.388 --> 00:02:15.515
When it's forced, the abdominal muscles and the internal intercostals contract.

43
00:02:15.965 --> 00:02:18.349
So the hook: breathing in, the neck lifts.

44
00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:21.811
Breathing out, the abs and the internal intercostals squeeze.

45
00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:23.989
<v Examiner>Three marks.

46
00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:27.997
<v Examiner>What are some of the factors that influence pulmonary ventilation,

47
00:02:27.997 --> 00:02:31.154
<v Examiner>making it easier or harder, and how do they do this?

48
00:02:31.604 --> 00:02:35.273
The hook: blocked airways, stiff lungs, and a lost pleural seal.

49
00:02:35.723 --> 00:02:36.851
Blocked airways.

50
00:02:36.851 --> 00:02:42.771
Obstruction by fluid, mucus or swelling, as in COPD and asthma, narrows the airways.

51
00:02:42.771 --> 00:02:45.449
That raises the resistance to airflow,

52
00:02:45.449 --> 00:02:49.185
and the muscular effort needed to breathe in and out.

53
00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:50.441
Stiff lungs.

54
00:02:50.441 --> 00:02:56.086
Lung compliance is the ease with which the lungs expand, and scar tissue reduces it.

55
00:02:56.086 --> 00:03:02.134
So do problems with the thoracic cage, like an inflexible rib cage or a diaphragm problem.

56
00:03:02.134 --> 00:03:07.846
And so do problems in the alveoli: a lack of surfactant, so the alveoli don't expand,

57
00:03:07.846 --> 00:03:09.795
or fluid building up in them.

58
00:03:10.245 --> 00:03:11.289
The pleural seal.

59
00:03:11.289 --> 00:03:16.142
The negative pressure in the pleural space couples the lungs to the chest wall.

60
00:03:16.142 --> 00:03:21.302
Air in the pleural cavity, a pneumothorax, breaks that seal, and the lung collapses.

61
00:03:21.302 --> 00:03:22.469
That's atelectasis.

62
00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:23.507
<v Examiner>Once more.

63
00:03:23.507 --> 00:03:24.625
<v Examiner>Manaia's breathing.

64
00:03:27.075 --> 00:03:31.140
Inhalation: the diaphragm and external intercostals contract,

65
00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:36.871
the thoracic cavity enlarges, the intrapleural and then intrapulmonary pressures fall,

66
00:03:36.871 --> 00:03:40.803
by Boyle's law, and air flows in from high pressure to low.

67
00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:46.800
Exhalation: they relax, the elastic lungs recoil passively, intrapulmonary pressure rises,

68
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.999
and air flows out.

69
00:03:47.999 --> 00:03:52.131
Forced breathing in adds the sternocleidomastoid and scalenes;

70
00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:57.062
forced breathing out uses the abdominal muscles and internal intercostals.

71
00:03:57.062 --> 00:04:00.061
Ventilation gets harder with blocked airways,

72
00:04:00.061 --> 00:04:05.458
with stiff lungs from scar tissue or no surfactant, and with a lost pleural seal:

73
00:04:05.458 --> 00:04:07.724
a pneumothorax collapses the lung.
